  persist across sessions" reads most naturally as that page remembering something. Adding a Node backend later is
  cheap; building the abstraction now is speculative.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which surface needs preferences storage? → Browser only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Browser only it is — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.

  Next: [1mwhat actually gets stored?[0m This sets the schema and how much UI work comes with it, and the page currently has
  exactly one feature (the login form) plus no settings controls at all.

  - [1mRemembered username[0m — the natural fit for what's on the page. Needs one checkbox in the form. To be explicit up
    front: I will not store the password or anything derived from it in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, regardless of what else we decide
    — it's readable by any script on the origin. If you want "stay logged in" later, that's a session token from a real
    backend, and this app's login is still a stub.
  - [1mDisplay preferences (theme, etc.)[0m — the conventional "settings" reading, but it means inventing a settings UI and
    something for the theme to actually affect. More net-new surface than storage work.
  - [1mJust the storage layer, no preferences yet[0m — a tested [38;5;153mpreferences.js[39m module with get/set/remove and a defaults map,
    wired to nothing. Useful if you have specific settings in mind for later and want the foundation first.

  [1mMy recommendation: remembered username, built on a small general preferences module.[0m It gives the storage layer a real
  consumer so we're not designing an API against imagined use, and the module stays open for whatever you add next.
